1. Cloud Deployment Models

A deployment model answers:

"Where is the cloud infrastructure deployed, and who has access to it?"

There are mainly 4 deployment models:

Public Cloud
Private Cloud
Hybrid Cloud
Community Cloud
☁️ 1. Public Cloud

In a public cloud, the infrastructure is owned and operated by a cloud provider and is made available to customers.

Examples:

AWS
Azure
Google Cloud
Real-time example

Suppose you start a small shopping website.

You don't want to buy servers.

You create:

AWS
 |
 ├── EC2
 ├── S3
 ├── RDS
 └── Load Balancer

Your application runs completely on AWS.

Simple analogy

Think about a rented apartment.

Apartment Owner
      |
      ↓
    You rent
      |
      ↓
    You use it

You don't own the building.

Similarly:

AWS owns infrastructure
        ↓
You rent/use resources
When useful?
Startups
Websites
Mobile applications
Dev/Test environments
Most modern cloud applications
🏢 2. Private Cloud

A private cloud is dedicated to one organization.

Example:

A large bank doesn't want certain workloads running on shared public-cloud infrastructure.

It can operate cloud infrastructure dedicated to the organization.

             BANK
              |
       ┌──────┴──────┐
       | Private     |
       | Cloud       |
       |             |
       | Servers     |
       | Storage     |
       | Network     |
       └─────────────┘
Simple analogy

Public cloud:

🏨 Hotel room — infrastructure is shared among different customers.

Private cloud:

🏠 Your own house — dedicated to you.

Common use cases

Organizations with strict:

Security requirements
Compliance requirements
Data-control requirements
Internal infrastructure requirements
🔀 3. Hybrid Cloud

This is a combination of:

Private Cloud + Public Cloud

Real example:

A bank keeps sensitive customer systems in its private environment but uses AWS for some applications, backups, analytics, or additional capacity.

              Company
                 |
       ┌─────────┴─────────┐
       ↓                   ↓
 Private Cloud          AWS
       |                   |
 Sensitive             Web/App
 systems              workloads
       \                   /
        \_________________/
              Network
Real-life analogy

Imagine you have:

🏠 Your own house → Private Cloud

🏨 Hotel → Public Cloud

You use both depending on your requirement.

👥 4. Community Cloud

Infrastructure is shared by organizations with similar requirements or interests.

For example, several organizations in a particular regulated sector could use infrastructure designed around common compliance/security requirements.

Organization A ──┐
Organization B ──┼── Community Cloud
Organization C ──┘

This model is less commonly discussed than public, private, and hybrid cloud.

📌 Deployment Models — Easy Table
Model	Infrastructure	Example
Public	Cloud provider	AWS
Private	Dedicated to one organization	Bank's private cloud
Hybrid	Public + Private	Bank + AWS
Community	Shared by organizations with common requirements	Sector-specific cloud
Remember:
PUBLIC    → Everyone/customer-accessible
PRIVATE   → One organization
HYBRID    → Public + Private
COMMUNITY → Organizations with common requirements
2. Types of Cloud Computing

Now a different question:

"What type of service are you getting from the cloud?"

The major service models are:

IaaS
PaaS
SaaS

You can think of them as how much infrastructure you have to manage yourself.

🖥️ IaaS — Infrastructure as a Service

You get infrastructure such as:

Virtual machines
Storage
Networking

Example:

AWS EC2

You create an EC2 instance:

EC2
 |
 ├── CPU
 ├── RAM
 ├── Storage
 └── Network

You are responsible for things such as:

Application
Runtime
OS
Configuration

while AWS manages the underlying physical infrastructure.

Real-world example

Suppose you rent an empty apartment.

The building exists, but you need to arrange much of what's inside.

AWS
 ↓
Infrastructure
 ↓
YOU
 ↓
OS
Application
Configuration
Examples
AWS EC2
Azure Virtual Machines
Google Compute Engine
⚙️ PaaS — Platform as a Service

Here the cloud provider manages more of the underlying platform.

You primarily focus on your application.

Conceptually:

       YOU
        ↓
  Application
        ↓
     Platform
        ↓
 Infrastructure
Real-world example

Suppose you want to deploy a web application.

Instead of:

Create VM
 ↓
Install OS
 ↓
Install runtime
 ↓
Configure runtime
 ↓
Deploy application

a PaaS offering can abstract much of that infrastructure/platform management.

Analogy

Instead of renting an empty apartment, imagine renting a fully equipped workspace.

You bring your work.

The provider handles much more of the environment.

📱 SaaS — Software as a Service

Here you simply use the software.

You don't manage the underlying servers or operating system.

Examples:

Gmail
Microsoft 365
Salesforce

For example:

You
 ↓
Open Gmail
 ↓
Send Email

You don't care:

Which server?
Which OS?
Which storage?
Which database?

The service provider handles those details.

🧠 IaaS vs PaaS vs SaaS

This is a very common interview question.

	IaaS	PaaS	SaaS
You manage	More	Less	Very little
Main focus	Infrastructure + app	Application	Using software
Example	EC2	App platform/service	Gmail
Flexibility	High	Medium	Lower
Infrastructure management	More	Less	Provider handles it
Easy memory trick
IaaS → I get Infrastructure
PaaS → I get Platform
SaaS → I get Software
💰 3. Cloud Pricing

Now comes another important concept:

How does cloud cost money?

The basic idea is:

Pay for what you use

Instead of buying a physical server for ₹5 lakh upfront, you can provision cloud resources and incur charges based on the service, configuration, usage, and pricing model.
